Short:        MUI-compatible GUI library, lean edition (test)
Author:       The AROS Development Team, Stefan Reinauer (Zune68), Zune68k lean contributors
Type:         util/libs
Version:      [RELEASE] - [STAGE]
Architecture: m68k-amigaos >= 3.1
Requires:     AmigaOS 3.1+, 68000+, Installer 43+

Zune68k lean is a muimaster.library for AmigaOS 3.x: AROS's Zune, as
ported to AmigaOS 3 by Zune68, with a memory and size diet so that it
runs on a stock 68000 with OS 3.1 and stays close to MUI 3.8 in memory.

This is a test build ([STAGE], [RELEASE]). Every figure below was
measured in an emulator (FS-UAE, A1200 with OS 3.1), not on a real Amiga.

What is in it
-------------
- muimaster.library [VERSION] and zunemaster.library (the same library
  under a second name, which the Zune preferences program uses).
- About 45 rarely used classes and parts of the library as files of their
  own in LIBS:Zune, loaded the first time a program needs one.
- The Zune preferences program (SYS:Prefs/Zune).
- [PLUGINS] MUI classes: NList, NListtree, TextEditor, BetterString,
  TheBar, Busy, Listtree and others, with their preference pages.
- Translations, an SDK with examples, and the full source.

Each program gets the MUI it asks for
-------------------------------------
A program written for MUI 3 sees MUI 3.8 (19.35), one asking for MUI 4
sees MUI 4, one asking for MUI 5 sees MUI 5 (21.227), and Zune behaves
accordingly. In Zune prefs, System page, "MUI version": "Behave as"
Automatic (the default), MUI 3.8, MUI 4 or MUI 5 for all programs, and a
list of programs with a MUI of their own.

Installing
----------
Double-click Install (Installer 43 or newer: on OS 3.1 use Installer
43.3 from Aminet, OS 3.2 has its own). The installer:
- looks for a MUI you already have (MUI 3.x, 4 or 5, its MUI: assign,
  its S:User-Startup lines, every muimaster.library in LIBS:) and tells
  you what it found. None of it is changed: Zune goes into your first
  LIBS: drawer (normally SYS:Libs), where programs find it before MUI,
  and nothing is added to S:Startup-Sequence or S:User-Startup;
- keeps every file it replaces, and writes down every file it installs,
  in SYS:Zune68k/Data;
- installs a bundled MUI class only when you do not have the same or a
  newer one yourself. The classes that come with a real MUI stay as they
  are for that MUI; Zune uses its own;
- keeps your Zune settings (ENVARC:Zune) on an update, and does not
  touch MUI's (ENVARC:MUI).
Novice, Intermediate and Expert levels and pretend mode work.

Switching and removing
----------------------
SYS:Zune68k has "Use real MUI" and "Use Zune" (when a real MUI was found)
and "Uninstall". "Use real MUI" puts Zune's libraries and classes aside
and puts your own files back: after a reboot programs get your MUI.
"Use Zune" switches back. "Uninstall" (or Install again, "Remove
Zune68k") deletes Zune's files and puts back every file of yours it had
replaced; your MUI keeps working. The same from a Shell:
SYS:Zune68k/Data/ZuneSwitch MUI|ZUNE|UNINSTALL|STATUS.

Status
------
A test build. MUI 3.8's 19 demos and real MUI programs (YAM, AmIRC,
AmFTP, IBrowse and others, see Compatibility) are tested against MUI 3.8
in FS-UAE; 30 of 36 MUI 3 programs behave as with MUI 3.8 there. MUI-Demo
on OS 3.1 with 4 MB fast RAM (FS-UAE): about 465 KB for the first program
(MUI 3.8: 460 KB), about 205 KB for each further one (MUI 3.8: 211-219
KB). Not yet measured on a real Amiga. Please report what works and what
does not.

